 ],
 "literal": "Fat-dripping quail as seasoned dish / may they obtain abundant food, O friend, indeed! / The lord of the big mountain country, because he agreed to marry, before him / 'Is it good, O son?' I asked. / 'It is good indeed, it seems,' he spoke.",
 "translation": "O friend! May they receive a rich feast of fat-dripping quail seasoned with spices! When the lord of the great mountain country agreed to marry you, I asked the diviner in his presence, 'Is this good, O son?' and he replied, 'It seems very good indeed!'",
 "modern": "தோழியே! கொழுப்பு நிறைந்த காடைப் பறவையை மசாலா சேர்த்துச் சமைத்த உணவை அவர்கள் பெருவிருந்தாகப் பெறட்டும்! பெரிய மலைநாட்டிற்குரிய தலைவன் உன்னைத் திருமணம் செய்ய உடன்பட்டபோது, அவனுக்கு முன்னால் நான் குறிசொல்லும் வேலனிடம், 'மகனே, இது நல்லதா?' என்று கேட்டேன். அதற்கு அவன், 'மிகவும் நல்லது போலிருக்கிறது!' என்று கூறினான்.",
 "explanation": "This poem is spoken by the heroine's friend (tōḻi) to the heroine (talaivi) upon the successful settlement of their marriage. The hero, the lord of the great mountain country, has formally proposed, and the family has accepted. The friend joyfully wishes for a grand celebratory feast featuring fat-dripping quail seasoned with spices. She recalls how she asked the diviner (addressed affectionately as 'makaṉ' or son) in the hero's presence if this marriage was a good match. The diviner enthusiastically confirmed that it was indeed excellent, removing all anxieties. This happy resolution of secret love into marriage is a classic theme of the Kuriñci (mountain) landscape.",
